        {
          "id": "the_two_HC_speciation_lookups_key_on_the_fuel_subtype_and_on_the_output_pollutant_process",
          "description": "`HCSpeciationCalculator`'s two tables, and the two different things their keys do. `methanethcratio` is keyed by (processID, fuelSubtypeID, regClassID) and a model-year band, and it fires on EVERY rate row of this run because all six share process 1 -- what selects the NMHC row is `rt_isNMHC`, the one pollutant this document names, because hcspeciation.rs names 79 as a constant too. `hcspeciation` is keyed by the OUTPUT pollutant-process, so joining a rate row's own `polProcessID` to it both finds the constants AND identifies which rows are VOC rows: no pollutant 87 is written anywhere in this document. Both lookups are INNER, and fuel subtype 90 is in neither table -- that single absence is every species block's twenty missing cohorts. `oxySpeciation` is 0 on all 136 rows, so `speciation_factor` never leaves its constant.\n\nONLY THE NMHC FLAG IS STILL ASSERTED (1 on the NMHC block, 0 on THC and VOC). The per-row methane ratios, speciation constants and presence flags were `rtSup_*` variables that are gone from the model, so the two lookups themselves are now checked only through the emitted NMHC and VOC cells.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 657
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 821
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_E85_altTHC_branch_speciates_VOC_from_a_different_fuel_than_it_burns",
          "description": "adjust.rs:625-692 and hcspeciation.rs:715-760, and it is worth a factor of 3.85. On an ethanol cohort of model year 2001 or later, MOVES duplicates the THC block of the supplied E85 formulation, scales the copy by `altRatio / ratio` -- 1.037215919005 / 1.044099696019 = 0.99340697 at model year 2020 -- and speciates THAT into VOC using fuel subtype 12's ratios, while the emitted NMHC still comes from the ordinary block and subtype 51's ratio of 0.822. So the VOC multiplier on this cohort's THC is 0.99340697 x (1 - 0.338) x 0.974 = 0.6405369, where the ordinary path would have given (1 - 0.822) x 0.934 = 0.1662520. The model year 2000 ethanol cohort has no `altcriteriaratio` row and takes the ordinary path, and a branch that fired everywhere and a branch that fired nowhere would both be wrong and only one of them would look it.\n\nONLY ONE INTERMEDIATE IS STILL ASSERTED: the E10 speciation constant 0.974 on the alt-THC row. The five conditions, the two criteria ratios and the VOC factors were variables that are gone from the model, so the branch is now checked through the E85 VOC cells of MOVESOutput in `the_two_air_toxic_paths_and_the_worked_examples_reach_MOVESOutput`.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_speciationConstantE10",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.974,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 903
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_two_air_toxic_paths_and_the_worked_examples_reach_MOVESOutput",
          "description": "`AirToxicsCalculator`'s two live paths and the end of the chain. ATRatioGas1 keys on the emission's fuel FORMULATION and on an ageID that resolves the model year -- gasoline at ageID 40 takes 0.037092331652 and at ageID 0 takes 0.047131980205, out of two windows that partition the run's model years -- and ATRatioNonGas keys on the fuel SUBTYPE and a self-described model-year group. The two are disjoint on every emitted cohort here, so the ratio is a sum of one term; the sum is written because the Go APPENDS both. A zero RATIO and a missing one are different: 1,3-butadiene on gasoline model year 2020 has a row whose value is 0 and is emitted at 0, while every species on electricity has no row and is not emitted at all. The ratios themselves are no longer variables and are not asserted -- what is asserted is the two ageIDs that select the gasoline windows, and the emitted cells. Then twenty-five `MOVESOutput` cells across all six pollutants, all four fuels, both day types and both ends of the model-year range, including the E85 rows the altTHC branch decides and the four cells that are exactly zero. `output_rows` is rate-major over the 644 emitting rate rows, one day type each, so row 41 is the model-year-1980 gasoline THC cell. It was 1,288 rows in blocks of TWO day types, weekend first, before the day correction (docs/esm-conventions.md 42).",
